#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a7899e04-8be9-597a-a9b6-111201ebad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8607" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ on the line segment $\\overline {AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x, y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a =  \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ (9-{2}^{2}-{1}^{2})-(9-{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,-2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =-4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{1-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1,c_2\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $4c_1 + 2c_2 = 5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+2(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ 5{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $c_2 = \\dfrac{c_1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $\\left(1, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2b224972-6a43-5691-9e46-abff577311d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8608" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 7-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1,-2z⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =4{c}_{1}+3+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{3}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t , \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t , \\quad c_2 = 3t , \\quad c_3 = 1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2t)+2(1-t) & =5 \\\\ 8t+2-2t & =5 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1,$ $c_2,$ and $c_3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0368b61d-2d85-58af-9af0-ec09edf877b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8693" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c?$\n\nI. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n\nII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n\nIII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨4,2⟩-⟨5,3⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nf_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) =-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "843a8cdb-f7ab-5034-ba5b-f2c3c40973aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121164} {:answer/id #uuid "04800849-bc31-5808-92fb-6910f5d59565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2})$ and $(1, 1, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121165} {:answer/id #uuid "08819b39-bfcd-55d5-855b-ccd7416a1ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/iden